==English==
{{wikipedia|lang=en}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*glew-}}
{{inh|en|enm|clewe}}, from {{inh|en|ang|cleowen}}, {{m|ang|cliewen}}, {{m|ang|cliwen||sphere, ball, skein; ball of thread or yarn; mass, group}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*kliuwiną}}, {{m|gem-pro|*klewô||ball, bale}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*glew-||to conglomerate, gather into a mass; clump, ball, bale}}. Akin to {{cog|ang|clǣġ||clay}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/kluː/|/kluʊ̯/}}
* {{a|obsolete}} {{IPA|en|/kljuː/}}<ref>{{R:Critical Pronouncing Dictionary|entry=Clew|page=166|ppage=1}}</ref>
* {{rhymes|en|uː|s=1}}
* {{homophones|en|clue|clou}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A roughly spherical mass or body.
#* '''c. 1600''', [[w:Charles Estienne|Charles Estienne]] and [[w:Jean Liebault|Jean Liebault]], tr. Richard Surflet, ''Maison Rustique, or, The Countrie Farme'':
#*: If the whole troupe be diuided into many '''clewes''', or round bunches, you need not then doubt but that there are many kings.
#* {{RQ:Stedman Surinam|volume=I|chapter=VII|page=153|passage=Both theſe creatures [the "ai" ([[aye-aye]]?) and "unan"], by forming themſelves in a '''clew''', have often more the appearance of excreſcences in the bark, than that of animals.}}
# {{senseid|en|yarn}}{{lb|en|archaic}} A ball of [[thread]] or [[yarn]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare All's Well|act=I|scene=iii|page=234|column=1|passage=[O]nely ſinne<br>And helliſh obſtinacie tye thy tongue<br>That truth ſhould be ſuſpected, ſpeake, iſ't ſo?<br>If it be ſo, you haue wound a goodly '''clewe''':<br>If it be not, forſweare't how ere I charge thee,<br>As heauen ſhall work in me for thine auaile<br>To tell me truelie.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1831|translator=[[w:Isabel Florence Hapgood|Isabel Florence Hapgood]]|author=[[w:Victor Hugo|Victor Hugo]]|title=[[The Hunchback of Notre Dame/Book First/Chapter III|The Hunchback of Notre Dame]]
|passage=A rare, precious, and never interrupted race of philosophers to whom wisdom, like another Ariadne, seems to have given a '''clew''' of thread which they have been walking along unwinding since the beginning of the world, through the labyrinth of human affairs.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1889|author=[[w:Andrew Lang|Andrew Lang]]|title=[[s:The Blue Fairy Book|The Blue Fairy Book]]|chapter=[[s:The story of Prince Ahmed and the fairy Paribanou|The story of Prince Ahmed and the fairy Paribanou]]
|passage=The Fairy Paribanou was at that time very hard at work, and, as she had several '''clews''' of thread by her, she took up one, and, presenting it to Prince Ahmed, said: "First take this '''clew''' of thread...}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1962|author={{w|Vladimir Nabokov}}|title=Pale Fire|passage=on one side of her lay a pair of carpet slippers and on the other a ball of red wool, the leading filament of which she would tug at every now and then with the immemorial elbow jerk of a Zemblan knitter to give a turn to her yarn '''clew''' and slacken the thread.}}
# [[yarn|Yarn]] or [[thread]] as used to guide one's way through a [[maze]] or [[labyrinth]]; a guide, a [[clue]].
# {{lb|en|nautical}} The [[lower]] [[corner]](s) of a [[sail]] to which a [[sheet]] is attached for [[trimming]] the sail (adjusting its position relative to the wind); the [[metal]] [[loop]] or [[cringle]] in the corner of the sail, to which the [[sheet]] is attached. {{lb|en|on a triangular sail}} The [[trail]]ing corner relative to the wind direction.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1858|author=Walter Mitchell|title=[[s:Tacking Ship Off Shore|Tacking Ship Off Shore]]|passage='Mid the rattle of blocks and the tramp of the crew,<br />Hisses the rain of the rushing squall;<br />The sails are aback from '''clew''' to '''clew''',<br />And now is the moment for "MAINSAIL, HAUL!"}}
#* '''1858''', ''The Atlantic Monthly'', "[[s:The Atlantic Monthly/Volume 2/No. 5/The Language of the Sea|The Language of the Sea]]":
#*: "'''Clew'''" is Saxon; "garnet" (from granato, a fruit) is Italian,—that is, the garnet- or pomegranate-shaped block fastened to the '''clew''' or corner of the courses, and hence the rope running through the block.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1894|author=[[w:Hudson Taylor|James Hudson Taylor]]|title=[[s:A Retrospect|A Retrospect]]|passage=I went over and asked him to let down the '''clews''' or corners of the mainsail, which had been drawn up in order to lessen the useless flapping of the sail against the rigging.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1901|author={{w|John Conroy Hutcheson}}|title=[[s:The Ghost Ship|The Ghost Ship]]|passage="Run aft, Haldane, and you too, Spokeshave. Loosen the bunt of the mizzen-trysail and haul at the '''clew'''. That’ll bring her up to the wind fast enough, if the sail only stands it!"}}
# {{lb|en|in the plural}} The sheets so attached to a sail.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1913|author=[[w:John Masefield|John Masefield]]|title=[[s:Dauber|Dauber]]
|passage=The canvas running up in a proud sweep,<br />Wind-wrinkled at the '''clews''', and white like lint,}}
# {{lb|en|nautical|in the plural}} The [[cord]]s [[suspend]]ing a [[hammock]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2000|author=Ralph W Danklefsen|title=The Navy I Remember|page=21|publisher=Xlibris
|passage=He taught us how to attach the '''clews''' to the ends of the hammock and then lash it between jack stays.}}
# {{obsolete spelling of|en|clue}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1766|author={{w|Laurence Sterne}}|title=The Sermons of Mr. Yorick|passage=With this '''clew''', let us endeavour to unravel this character of Herod as here given.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1841|author={{w|Edgar Allan Poe}}|title=The Murders in the Rue Morgue|passage=To this horrible mystery there is not as yet, we believe, the slightest '''clew'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1848|author={{w|Thomas Babington Macaulay}}|title={{w|The History of England from the Accession of James the Second|The History of England from the Accession of James II}}|volume=III,1856|publisher=Harper & Brothers|location=New York|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=rWINAAAAIAAJ&pg=PA13|page=13
|passage=The '''clew''', without which it was perilous to enter the vast and intricate maze of Continental politics, was in his hands.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1864|author={{w|James Redding Ware|Andrew Forrester}}|title=The Female Detective|passage=Now, the fact is, I had started because I thought I saw the end of a good '''clew'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1870|author={{w|Edward Augustus Freeman}}|title=History of the Norman Conquest|passage=We may here have lighted on the '''clew''' to the great puzzle.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1910|chapter=Duck Eats Yeast,|title=The Yakima Herald
|passage=Telltale marks around the pan of yeast gave him a '''clew''' to the trouble.}}
#* {{RQ:Burroughs Princess of Mars|chapter=A Fight that Won Friends|page=59|passage=They had followed immediately behind him, thinking it barely possible that his actions might prove a '''clew''' to my whereabouts, and had witnessed my short but decisive battle with him.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1923|author=[[w:Robert E. Howard|Robert Ervin Howard]]|title=[[s:Aha! or The Mystery of the Queen's Necklace|Aha! or The Mystery of the Queen's Necklace]]|passage=And I brought the only '''clew''' to be found.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1926|author=Robertus Love|title=The Rise and Fall of Jesse James|publisher=University of Nebraska|year_published=1990
|passage=Not often did Jesse James leave a '''clew''' to his identity when he galloped away from a crime of violence, back into the mysterious Nowhere whence he came.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1954|author={{w|Robert Heinlein}}|title={{w|The Star Beast}}|publisher=New English Library
|passage=following the single '''clew''' that she must have gone off with a certain group of visitors from space; they knew what those visitors looked like but not from what part of the sky they came.}}

